<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiangxi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jiangxi is an inland province of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanchang">Nanchang</a>, centred on the basin of the Gan River and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-poyang">Poyang Lake</a>, which drain into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. A rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-growing region, it became famous for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> of Jingdezhen. From 1931 to 1934 its mountainous south was the heart of the Chinese Soviet Republic, from which the Communists set out on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiangxi.jpg" alt="Jiangxi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Emitchan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SanQingShan9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
